{ [%evp 0,69,91,86,87,85,71,70,85,76,71,61,77,60,81,63,85,68,77,72,76,67,94,64,82,68,87,68,93,58,76,87,83,44,96,55,74,91,70,70,86,58,94,76,99,75,84,89,118,61,89,97,123,87,94,92,32767,126,135,70,133,111,126,61,136,90,162,133,432,447,452,444] [#] }
29. Ba4
{ The situation before the game was the following: I knew that if I won this game, I would have a direct chance to play for a GM norm in the final round. So I wanted to win this game more than anything. However, after a good preparation, I missed the chance to gain a bigger advantage and very soon found myself in this endgame. I was already a bit frustrated, and I was seriously considering playing Bxe8 and trying out the 4-vs-3 pawn endgame, while more or less accepting the draw. But then I remembered the principle of “Do Not Rush” and realized that I could postpone that decision for quite some time. I even consciously thought: “I’m literally just going to annoy him by waiting and doing nothing.” My opponent, however, was completely unfazed by this strategy. He simply matched my energy and also did nothing. What he missed, though, was the crucial moment when I switched from stalling to improving. With an insane number of Kg7–Kg8 waiting moves from Black, I gradually improved my position without my opponent realizing. Eventually, I managed to win. }
29... h5 30. Kg2 g6 31. Kf3 Kg7 32. h4 Kg8 33. Ra7 Kg7 34. Ke2 Kg8 35. Rc5 Kg7
36. Rc4 Kg8 37. Rc6 Kg7 38. Rc3 Kg8 39. Kd3 Kg7 40. Rc4 Kg8 41. Rc5 Kg7 42. Rc6
Rd7+ 43. Ke2 Re7 44. Kf3 Re5 45. Rc4 Re7 46. Rc2 Kg8 47. Ke2 Kg7 48. Rc3 Kg8
49. Rc5 Kg7 50. Bc2 Kg8 51. f3 Kg7 52. Bd3 ( 52. e4 $1 $14
{ to prevent f5 would have already been an slight edge for white. } ) 52... Kg8
53. e4 $14 Kg7 54. f4 $6 { letting black escape by improving his knight. } (
54. Bc2 $1
{ [%cal Ge2f2,Ge4e5] preparing the e5 adavance without allowing any counterplay. }
) 54... Kg8 ( 54... Nf6 $1 55. e5 Rc8 56. Ba6 Rce8 { [%cal Gf6g8,Gg8h6,Gh6f5] }
) 55. Kf3 Kg7 56. Bc2 Kg8 57. e5 Kg7 58. Ba4 Kg8 59. Rc3 Kg7 60. Ra8 Kg8 $4
{ thats too much. The game is lost. } ( 60... c5 $1
{ [%csl Yc7][%cal Ge8c7] vacating the c7-square for the knight would have given black an 0.00 again, however not a draw yet. }
) 61. Rd3 { [%csl Re8,Rf8,Rg8][%cal Gd3d8] } 61... c6 ( 61... Ng7 62. Rxf8+
Kxf8 63. Rd8+ Ne8 64. Bd7 { and black is completely paralized. } ) 62. Rdd8 Re6
63. Rac8 1-0

